# What a derivation is handed and what it may answer (#2186).
#
# An entry that writes `derive conventions/routes` has the compiler call that
# function, during the build, with a `program` describing the entry and the
# modules it imports. The function answers a list of declarations, and the
# compiler adds them to the entry before it checks anything, so a declaration
# is checked like code someone wrote.
#
# `declare` makes a constant, `name = value`, in the entry. The value is
# data: ints, strings, bools, lists, maps, records and references to
# functions, which is how a route table names its handlers and why the
# checker sees each one. `declare_type` makes a record type in the entry,
# named `name` with the listed fields. `define` adds an arm, written out as
# source, to the module `origin` names (`posts/post` puts it in `posts`). The
# module compiles it as one more of its own files, so the arm may claim the
# module's own types, and it is checked like code someone wrote there.
# `refuse` makes a compile error at the item `origin` names, which is how a
# convention reports a function it cannot place.
#
# The entry comes first in `modules`, under its file's name. It holds
# statements only, so it lists only its calls, and a convention that checks
# calls checks the ones the entry writes too.
#
# `files` maps the name of each file directly beside the entry that is not
# kanso source and reads as text to that text, such as a `schema.sql` a
# convention reads. A compile with no directory under it, as in the tab, is
# handed none.
pub type program
  modules
  files

# `constants` maps each pub constant's name to its value, which is how a
# derivation reads data the app keeps, such as its migrations.
# `calls` lists every call a function in the module makes to a named
# function, in the order they are written: the function that makes it, or ""
# for a call in the entry's statements; the function it calls; each
# argument's name, or "" for an argument that is not a name; and each
# argument's text, or "" for an argument that is not a string written whole.
# `refuse_call` makes a compile error at one of them.
pub type module
  name
  functions
  constants
  records
  calls

pub type call
  module
  caller
  callee
  arguments
  strings
  line
  column

# A pub function as its first arm declares it. An unannotated parameter
# carries "" for its annotation. `reference` is the function itself, so a
# table a derivation declares can hold it and call it.
pub type function
  name
  params
  reference

pub type param
  name
  annotation

pub type record
  name
  fields

pub type constant
  origin
  name
  value

pub type type_declaration
  origin
  name
  fields

pub type definition
  origin
  source

pub type refusal
  origin
  message

pub type call_refusal
  call
  message

pub fn declare origin name value
  constant origin name value

pub fn declare_type origin name fields
  type_declaration origin name fields

pub fn define origin source
  definition origin source

pub fn refuse origin message
  refusal origin message

pub fn refuse_call c message
  call_refusal c message
